Around 2026 tagged \PDF\ was a bit of a hype and \AI\ an even bigger one. But as
with all \quote {at some point fancy} features and technology, one can't predict
if the hype is worth it. Just watch amazing tech from decades (or ages) ago that
today is not around any more except in museums. With the two technologies above
it might be that the first becomes obsolete when the second evolves more, and
especially when these large language models become a useful and reliable tool.
We therefore want to explore the following hypothesis:

Can we forget about (often unreliable) tagging and delegate accessibility to
language models?

We will test this on some (simple) math and when we succeed we can assume (or
hope) that for ordinary typeset documents the same applies. And therefore we can
save us a lot of work, get less bloated files, where the bloat eventually gets
ignored anyway.
